      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $6x+y=3?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $6x+y=3,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+y & =3 \\\\ y & =-6x+3 \\\\ y & =-6x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-6.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{1}{6}x$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{1}{6}x$ is perpendicular to the given line.",
