      ":question/worked-solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $25x+5y=-14,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25x+5y & =-14 \\\\ 5y & =-25x-14 \\\\ y & =-5x-\\frac{14}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-5.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{1}{5}x-6$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{1}{5}x-6$ is perpendicular to the given line.",
